{"packet_id": "6933909ba20111ba", "dimensions": {"problem_framing": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A1"], "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether public evidence justifies a consumer proposition) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: B2B expansion, no expansion, retail partnership and licensing. It also sets constraints on consumer reach and value capture. It does not identify the constraint a good analyst would not want missed: the brand's fire-safety liability after Grenfell, which is present in the dossier."}, "causal_mechanism": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "T2 (A10) explains who changes behaviour (energy-cost-sensitive homeowners), why they change (lower bills), and how value is captured (volume and margin). The chain is generic. It never traces how Kingspan would capture value through a real consumer channel. The dossier shows only a third-party online marketplace listing (kingspan-bq-marketplace). T1 (A9) treats the Kingspan brand as a fire-safety asset without addressing kingspan-grenfell-k15."}, "distinct_theories": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "T1 and T2 use the same template: homeowners buy the same Kingspan insulation through the same unspecified channel, motivated by a different benefit. They differ only in messaging. There is no rival covering low consumer demand, professional-install dependence, or channel or partner models, so they are close to rewordings."}, "assumptions_predictions": {"status": "scored", "score": 2, "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"], "reason": "The predictions are observable: willingness to pay at least 15% or 10% more, and purchase intent by label. They are not strongly falsifying. The thresholds are arbitrary assumptions. The regional sales predictions confound building codes and heating costs with many other factors. Failure would not clearly put either theory in trouble."}, "evidence_judgement": {"status": "sco